  "text": "The tide came in an hour early, not with the usual groan of saltwater against the jetty, but with the rhythmic, hydraulic hiss of industrial machinery. Elias didn’t look up from his ledger. He had stopped expecting the ocean to behave like a natural force three years ago, when the Corporation bought the coastline. Now, the 'tide' was a scheduled delivery of processed seawater, pumped from the deep-sea extraction plants to the coastal desalination hubs. It was precise, sterile, and utterly devoid of the chaotic beauty that had once defined the shore. The water didn’t crash; it flowed, a steady, gray ribbon of liquid currency, filling the basins that lined the beach where children used to build sandcastles. Now, the children built data towers out of recycled plastic, their laughter muffled by the hum of the filtration units. Elias marked the timestamp in his book. 08:00 AM. On time. The sky above was a uniform shade of industrial gray, filtered through the atmospheric scrubbers that kept the air breathable but the light dim. He remembered the last time he had seen a real wave, a wild, white-capped thing that had thrown him into the sand. It felt like a lifetime ago, a relic of a chaotic, unmanaged world. Now, everything was managed. The weather was scheduled. The seasons were optimized. Even the moonrise was adjusted to maximize solar panel efficiency. He closed the ledger and walked to the edge of the basin. The water was still, reflecting the towering spires of the city. It was perfect. It was safe. And it was completely, terrifyingly empty. He dipped his hand in. It was warm. It tasted like nothing. He pulled his hand out, wiped it on his trousers, and went back inside to prepare for the next shift. The tide would come in again in twelve hours. It always did. It had to."
