  "text": "Elias returned to his desk, the warmth of the sterile water still clinging to his fingertips like a phantom sensation. He opened the ledger to the next page, expecting the usual columns of flow rates and pressure metrics. Instead, the ink was wet. Not the dry, chemical stain of corporate print, but a dark, viscous smear that pulsed with a faint, bioluminescent rhythm. He frowned, leaning closer. The numbers were rearranging themselves, not into data, but into a map. A map of the old coastline, the one that had been paved over, buried, and forgotten. The lines were jagged, wild, and undeniably alive. A drop of the liquid slid off the page and hit the floor, sizzling as it touched the synthetic tile. From the sizzle, a single, green shoot erupted, piercing the sterile surface. It grew with terrifying speed, twisting into a vine that wrapped around his wrist. Elias didn’t scream. He didn’t pull away. He watched, mesmerized, as the vine bloomed into a flower that smelled of salt and ozone, the scent of the world before the Corporation. The filtration units outside began to shudder, not with the hum of machinery, but with the roar of a storm. The gray sky cracked, revealing not the sun, but a swirling vortex of clouds that defied the atmospheric scrubbers. The tide was coming in, but it was no longer a delivery. It was a reclamation. Elias smiled, the flower’s petals brushing against his cheek, and for the first time in three years, he felt the chaotic, beautiful terror of being alive."
}

