  "text": "Elias’s smile didn’t fade; it hardened into a grimace of professional disappointment. The vine didn’t bloom; it snapped. With a wet, fibrous crack, the organic matter disintegrated into gray ash, drifting down onto his pristine white shirt. The bioluminescent pulse on the ledger page flickered and died, the ink drying instantly into a dull, brown stain that looked suspiciously like a coffee spill. The roar of the storm outside cut off abruptly, replaced by the low, rhythmic thrum of the HVAC system kicking back into overdrive. The gray sky didn’t crack; it remained a flat, unyielding sheet of concrete, the atmospheric scrubbers humming their eternal, soul-crushing lullaby. Elias sighed, the chaotic terror replaced by the familiar, heavy weight of boredom. He reached for his stylus, not to draw, but to correct the error. The 'map' was just a glitch in the new firmware, a rendering bug in the augmented reality overlay he had forgotten to disable. The 'vine' was a haptic feedback malfunction, a ghost in the machine that had briefly simulated touch. He tapped the screen, clearing the anomaly. The numbers returned to their orderly columns of flow rates and pressure metrics. The sterile water on his fingertips was just water, cold and indifferent. He picked up his coffee, the mug warm and real, and took a sip. The taste was bitter, synthetic, and perfectly safe. Outside, the city continued its silent, gray rotation, untouched by tides or storms, a monument to the successful eradication of the wild. Elias opened the next page, ready to log the incident as a minor system fluctuation, already forgetting the scent of salt and ozone that had, for one impossible second, threatened to drown him in memory."
